= Memorandum Rubicon

Since the memorandum of March 12, 1971 was issued by the Turkish Armed Forces and forced the resignation of Süleyman Demirel’s government, the republic has effectively been under military control. However, unlike the 1960 coup, this time around the generals have chosen to keep parliament and civilian institutions in place. Therefore, although parliament and elected politicians retain their positions, army made sure to constantly remind them where the true power lied in Ankara.

Following Demirel’s resignation, the formation of the new government fell to a former member of our party of great importance and merit, Nihat Erim, who had left the **CHP** to form a seemingly nonpartisan government. Erim was very driven at the beginning of his tenure, believing that now was finally his opportunity to make the changes in the country that previous governments had failed to make. Supported by professors, technocrats and experts, Erim’s government promised changes including land redistribution, economic development and social equality in order to provide stability.

Following the abduction and murder of Israeli Consul General Ephraim Elrom by the **THKP-C**, Operation Balyoz unleashed a widespread crackdown on Left movements and groups across the republic, with socialist intellectuals, journalists, trade unionists and students arrested and tortured, publications censored, universities brought under tighter control, and freedoms guaranteed by the 1961 Constitution increasingly curtailed.

Erim’s attempts to bring reform to Turkey were steadily stifled by the military after Operation Balyoz as they would make Erim and his government effectively non-important and took the matters to their hands and even before it **AP**'s filibuster behavior at every amendment and bill Erim's government would bring to the parliament had similarly severely killed the steam of Erim's government. By December, 11 of Erim’s ministers had resigned, bringing down his first cabinet. Erim was then tasked with forming another government, but the original cabinet of reformists was gone, instead chosen by the military behind the shadows, and Erim's reform agenda ended.

And things are not looking great for our party either... After condemning the Memorandum of March 12, 1971, highlighting the lack of democracy in having governments dismissed at the whim of military commanders, İsmet İnönü decided to later support the formation of a civilian government under Nihat Erim to prevent direct control of the country by the generals.

Meanwhile, Süleyman Demirel's **AP** endorsed constitutional changes under the military-backed government that increased executive powers and limited freedoms, cooperating closely with the generals.

However, Bülent Ecevit and the increasingly large Left of Center group disagreed with this approach, they argued that any agreement with the military in order to save parliamentary rule gave legitimacy to the intervention and therefore went against the interests of the people. As a result, Ecevit resigned as general secretary of the party. He was eventually replaced by Kâmil Kırıkoğlu. Alongside their allies, the Left of Center group seeks to move the **CHP** away from its traditional leadership.

The Rubicon of the memorandum has been reached. If İnönü is defeated in the coming leadership struggle, we will gain control of the **CHP**. However, in order to restore civilian rule, we must also challenge the generals' desire to control politics, their patience for democracy seems to be much less than their desire for power... In these next few months, **CHP** will either return to the people with the dove of democracy, or be the chief witness of the bayonet plunging at her republic's heart.

- @post_event: After almost a year of the military making decisions for us, it is finally time that we make a decision for ourselves...
